To merge two arrays in JavaScript, use either the spread operator or the concat method. Here are practical examples:

```javascript
// Using spread operator (modern, preferred)
const fruits = ['apple', 'banana'];
const vegetables = ['carrot', 'broccoli'];
const food = [...fruits, ...vegetables];
// Result: ['apple', 'banana', 'carrot', 'broccoli']

// Using concat method (traditional)
const merged = fruits.concat(vegetables);
// Result: ['apple', 'banana', 'carrot', 'broccoli']

// Merge and remove duplicates
const arr1 = [1, 2, 3];
const arr2 = [3, 4, 5];
const unique = [...new Set([...arr1, ...arr2])];
// Result: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

// Merge into existing array (in-place)
const target = [1, 2];
target.push(...[3, 4]);
// target is now [1, 2, 3, 4]
```

The spread operator syntax `[...a, ...b]` is the most readable for merging two or more arrays. Concat is a method call: `a.concat(b)`. Both produce shallow copies. If your arrays contain objects, both the original and merged arrays will reference the same objects, so mutating an object in the merged array affects the original. For merging more than two arrays, spread is especially clean: `[...a, ...b, ...c]`. For in-place merging, `push` with spread (`target.push(...source)`) modifies the target array without creating a new one. Choose the method that best fits your needs: spread/concat for new arrays, push with spread for in-place appending.

The `concat()` method in JavaScript is used to merge two or more arrays into a new array without modifying the original arrays. The syntax is: `const newArray = array1.concat(array2, array3, ...);`. It can also accept individual values: `array1.concat(4, 5, 6)`. Here are detailed examples:

```javascript
const letters = ['a', 'b', 'c'];
const numbers = [1, 2, 3];

// Merge two arrays
const combined = letters.concat(numbers);
// ['a', 'b', 'c', 1, 2, 3]

// Merge multiple arrays and values
const result = letters.concat(numbers, ['x', 'y'], 99);
// ['a', 'b', 'c', 1, 2, 3, 'x', 'y', 99]

// concat preserves the original arrays
console.log(letters); // ['a', 'b', 'c']

// Shallow copy behavior
const objArray = [{id: 1}, {id: 2}];
const more = objArray.concat([{id: 3}]);
more[0].id = 99;
console.log(objArray[0].id); // 99 — objects are shared
```

One important behavior of `concat` is that it only flattens one level: if you pass a nested array like `[[1, 2]]`, it adds the nested array as a single element, not its individual elements. For deeply nested structures, combine `concat` with `flat()` or use `flatMap`. While `concat` is widely supported even in very old JavaScript environments, modern code often prefers the spread operator (`...`) for readability. However, `concat` remains a valid choice, especially when chaining methods or when working with arrays that may contain non-array elements that should remain as-is. The method is chainable, meaning you can do `arr.concat(b).concat(c)` though multi-argument concat is cleaner.

To merge two arrays in JavaScript without duplicates, the most efficient and concise approach combines the spread operator with a Set:

```javascript
const arr1 = [1, 2, 3, 4];
const arr2 = [3, 4, 5, 6];

const merged = [...new Set([...arr1, ...arr2])];
// Result: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]
```

Here's how it works: `[...arr1, ...arr2]` creates a merged array that may contain duplicates. Wrapping it in `new Set(...)` removes all duplicates because a Set can only contain unique values. Then `[...set]` converts the Set back into an array. This approach works for primitive values (strings, numbers, booleans, null, undefined, symbols). For objects, Sets use reference equality, so two objects with identical properties are treated as different unless they are the same reference. For object deduplication, you would need a different approach, such as using `Map` or a custom filter with `findIndex`:

```javascript
const byId = [...new Map([...arr1, ...arr2].map(item => [item.id, item])).values()];
```

This creates a Map using `id` as the key, ensuring only one object per ID survives. If order matters, note that Set preserves insertion order, so the first occurrence of each value is kept. With objects, the Map approach keeps the last occurrence because Map overwrites keys. For primitive comparisons, the Set method is O(n) time complexity and very readable. For large arrays, you can also use `Array.from(new Set([...arr1, ...arr2]))` instead of the spread syntax to convert the Set back to an array.

The JavaScript spread operator (`...`) provides a clean, expressive way to merge arrays. Introduced in ES6, it unpacks an iterable (like an array) into individual elements. Here are various merging patterns using the spread operator:

```javascript
const teamA = ['Alice', 'Bob'];
const teamB = ['Charlie', 'Diana'];

// Basic merge
const allTeam = [...teamA, ...teamB];
// ['Alice', 'Bob', 'Charlie', 'Diana']

// Merge with additional elements inline
const extended = [...teamA, 'Eve', ...teamB];
// ['Alice', 'Bob', 'Eve', 'Charlie', 'Diana']

// Merge multiple arrays
const combined = [...teamA, ...teamB, ...['Frank', 'Grace']];

// Merge into an existing array (in-place, using push)
const existing = [1, 2];
existing.push(...[3, 4]);
// existing is now [1, 2, 3, 4]

// Create a copy before merging to avoid mutation
const original = [1, 2, 3];
const copy = [...original, 4, 5];
// original remains [1, 2, 3]
```

The spread operator creates shallow copies of the arrays, so nested objects are still referenced rather than deeply cloned. One key advantage of spread over `concat()` is that spread works with any iterable, not just arrays: `[...set, ...map.keys()]` is valid. Spread also works in function calls: `Math.max(...numbers)`. However, spread cannot be used in older browsers without a transpiler like Babel. In terms of performance, spread is comparable to `concat()` for most use cases, though it can be slower for extremely large arrays because it iterates the iterable explicitly. The spread operator is now the idiomatic JavaScript way to merge arrays in modern codebases, and it's frequently seen in React/Redux immutable update patterns.

JavaScript array manipulation encompasses many operations including merging, filtering, mapping, reducing, and more. For merging specifically, the key methods are `concat()`, the spread operator, and `push()` with spread. But merging is often combined with other manipulations. Here are common patterns:

```javascript
const arr1 = [1, 2, 3];
const arr2 = [4, 5, 6];

// Merge then transform
const doubled = [...arr1, ...arr2].map(n => n * 2);

// Filter duplicates while merging
const unique = [...new Set([...arr1, ...arr2])];

// Merge arrays of objects (add a property during merge)
const users1 = [{name: 'Alice'}, {name: 'Bob'}];
const users2 = [{name: 'Charlie'}];
const withRole = [...users1, ...users2].map(u => ({...u, role: 'user'}));

// Conditional merge
const shouldInclude = true;
const items = [
  ...arr1,
  ...(shouldInclude ? arr2 : []),
];

// Merge using reduce (custom logic)
const merged = [arr1, arr2].reduce((acc, curr) => [...acc, ...curr], []);

// In-place manipulation
const stack = [1, 2, 3];
stack.push(...[4, 5]); // Append
const last = stack.pop(); // Remove from end
stack.unshift(...[0]);  // Prepend
```

For more advanced manipulation, you might use `splice()` for inserting elements at specific indices, `slice()` for extracting portions, or `sort()` after merging. Modern JavaScript also provides `flat()` and `flatMap()` for handling nested arrays. When working with immutable state (like React), always prefer methods that return new arrays (spread, concat, map, filter) over mutating methods (push, splice, sort in-place). Array manipulation is a fundamental skill in JavaScript because arrays are the primary data structure for ordered collections, and most data processing involves merging, transforming, or filtering them.

Joining two arrays in JavaScript is commonly done with the spread operator (`[...arr1, ...arr2]`) or the `concat()` method (`arr1.concat(arr2)`). Both produce a new array containing all elements from both source arrays. Here are examples of joining arrays in different scenarios:

```javascript
const frontend = ['React', 'Vue', 'Angular'];
const backend = ['Node', 'Python', 'Go'];

// Method 1: Spread operator
const skills = [...frontend, ...backend];

// Method 2: concat()
const skills2 = frontend.concat(backend);

// Method 3: push with spread (mutates frontend)
frontend.push(...backend);

// Join with duplicate removal
const a = [1, 2, 3];
const b = [2, 3, 4];
const joined = [...new Set([...a, ...b])];
// [1, 2, 3, 4]

// Join at a specific position
const insertAt = 2;
const result = [...a.slice(0, insertAt), ...b, ...a.slice(insertAt)];
// [1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 3]

// Conditional join
const showExtra = true;
const list = ['item1', ...(showExtra ? ['item2', 'item3'] : [])];
```

If you need to join arrays of strings, you might also want to use `.join()` to create a single string — but that's a different operation (it concatenates array elements into a string, not arrays together). For joining arrays within a React component's state, the spread operator is idiomatic for immutable updates: `setItems(prev => [...prev, ...newItems])`. For performance considerations, if you're joining very large arrays repeatedly, consider whether a different data structure might be more appropriate. For most practical use cases, spread and concat are both efficient and readable, with spread being the more modern and widely adopted choice.

Merging arrays without duplicates in JavaScript is most elegantly done using the Set object, which inherently stores unique values. Here is a comprehensive guide:

```javascript
// Basic primitive deduplication
const arr1 = [1, 2, 3, 4];
const arr2 = [3, 4, 5, 6];
const merged = [...new Set([...arr1, ...arr2])];
// [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]

// Works with any primitive types
const a = ['apple', 'banana'];
const b = ['banana', 'cherry'];
const fruits = [...new Set([...a, ...b])];
// ['apple', 'banana', 'cherry']

// Mixed types (Set uses SameValueZero comparison)
const mixed = [...new Set([...['1', 1], ...[1, '2']])];
// ['1', 1, '2']

// For objects (reference equality only)
const objs1 = [{id: 1}, {id: 2}];
const objs2 = [{id: 2}, {id: 3}];
const mergedObjs = [...new Set([...objs1, ...objs2])];
// All 4 objects survive because they are different references

// Object deduplication by property using Map
const uniqueById = [...new Map(
  [...objs1, ...objs2].map(obj => [obj.id, obj])
).values()];
// [{id: 1}, {id: 2}, {id: 3}]

// Alternative using filter and findIndex (slower but explicit)
const all = [...arr1, ...arr2];
const deduped = all.filter((item, index) => all.indexOf(item) === index);
```

The Set approach is concise, readable, and has O(n) time complexity because Set lookups are O(1). It preserves insertion order (first occurrence is kept). For primitive arrays, this is the recommended approach. For objects where you need property-based deduplication, the Map technique shown above is efficient and also respects insertion order. The filter+indexOf approach is O(n^2), so avoid it for large arrays.

Both `concat()` and the spread operator (`...`) create new arrays when merging and have similar performance for typical use cases, but there are nuances. The spread operator essentially does what `concat` does but with explicit iteration: it creates a new array, then iterates each spread array and pushes each element. The `concat` method is a native C++ implementation in V8 and other engines, which gives it a slight theoretical advantage. In practice, for arrays with fewer than about 10,000 elements, the difference is negligible. Here are performance guidelines:

```javascript
// Small arrays (< 10k elements) — both are fine
const merged = [...small, ...small2];
const merged2 = small.concat(small2);

// Very large arrays — concat may be marginally faster
// due to native implementation
const huge1 = new Array(100000).fill(1);
const huge2 = new Array(100000).fill(2);
console.time('spread');
const s = [...huge1, ...huge2];
console.timeEnd('spread');
console.time('concat');
const c = huge1.concat(huge2);
console.timeEnd('concat');
```

Neither method should be used for arrays with hundreds of thousands of elements if performance is critical — consider using typed arrays or custom merge functions. For in-place merging with maximum performance, `push.apply(target, source)` or `Array.prototype.push.apply(target, source)` is the fastest because it pushes elements in bulk using the native C++ implementation. However, in modern code, readability usually matters more than micro-optimizations. The spread operator is preferred in most codebases because it's more readable and expressive. If you're doing a tight loop with millions of operations, then profile and consider concat or push.apply. For typical web development use cases, always choose readability first and optimize only when profiling shows a bottleneck.
